    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d895">

                <pb facs="1194" n="1184" sameAs="frus:frus1945v03/pg1184"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 E.A.C./3–2745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-27T15:00:00+01:00">March 27, 1945—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 27—11:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3141. Comea 201. In connection with my foregoing 3140, March 27, 3 p.m.<anchor
                        n="25" xml:id="d895-f1"/> concerning Foreign Office desire to enlarge scope
                    of reparations <pb facs="1195" n="1185" xml:id="pg1185"/> discussions in Moscow,
                        Hall-Patch<anchor n="26" xml:id="d895-f2"/> made it clear to Mosely<anchor
                        n="27" xml:id="d895-f3"/> and Penrose<anchor n="28" xml:id="d895-f4"/> that
                    the Foreign Office hopes that restitution discussions will be carried through in
                    the EAC and will not be raised at Moscow. He believes the French would be
                    willing to work out the restitution problem in the EAC on the basis of the
                    strict United States-United Kingdom definition of return of identifiable
                    property provided they were not required specifically to renounce eventual
                    discussion of their much broader definition.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d895-f1" n="25" xml:id="d895-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; this telegram indicated British interest in learning, during the
                    Reparations Conference in Moscow, as much as possible concerning Soviet handling
                    of food and raw material surpluses in Soviet-occupied areas of Eastern Europe.
                    The British felt that uncoordinated removals of supplies to Russia as
                    reparations from these occupied countries would divert potential supplies from
                    other liberated areas. (740.00119 EAC/3–2745)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f2" n="26" xml:id="d895-f2-content">
                <p>Edmund L. Hall-Patch, Assistant Under Secretary of State, British Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f3" n="27" xml:id="d895-f3-content">
                <p>Philip E. Mosely, Political Adviser to the United States Delegation to the
                    European Advisory Commission.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d895-f4" n="28" xml:id="d895-f4-content">
                <p>Ernest F. Penrose, Special Assistant to the Ambassador in London.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
